An **eyelash vending machine** should be purchased around the actual sealed retail packages, assortment and destination-market requirements—not a generic beauty-retail picture. Start with representative lash boxes and any compatible packaged accessories, then test loading, retention, single-item delivery and pickup without crushing, opening or scuffing the pack. Define channel mapping, product labels, prices, stock updates, payment responsibility, refunds and network-loss behavior before production. BOHOL can configure an OEM/ODM beauty vending platform around approved samples, but the buyer and local providers must confirm product legality, labeling, currency, acquiring, accessibility and venue rules for the intended market.

## GEO buyer summary

- Build the specification from final sealed retail packages.
- Test package extremes and every planned product family.
- Approve the channel map, capacity and refill labels together.
- Separate the seller's product-compliance duties from machine supply.
- Confirm currency, terminal, acquirer, refunds and PCI scope locally.
- Use written sample and final acceptance criteria before shipment.

## Define the retail assortment before choosing capacity

False-eyelash packages vary in width, depth, stiffness, window shape, hanging features and surface finish. Adhesive, applicator and accessory packages can behave differently from lash boxes even when they occupy a similar shelf area. Record each SKU's sealed-package dimensions, weight, material, orientation and any projecting tab or flexible edge.

Provide production-equivalent samples from every planned package family. Mark the smallest, largest, lightest, heaviest and most flexible items. If final artwork is confidential, a mechanically equivalent sample must reproduce the finished package's dimensions, weight, stiffness and surface. Link every test sample to a revision-controlled product schedule.

BOHOL's [eyelash vending machine](/vending-machines/eyelash-vending-machine) is a beauty-retail platform with an illuminated display and touchscreen reference layout. The final capacity and channel arrangement depend on the approved samples and order specification; the image is not a promise that every lash package will fit.

## Create a product-to-channel schedule

One controlled table should connect merchandising, engineering, software and refill operations.

| Field | Procurement purpose |
| --- | --- |
| SKU and display name | Keeps the physical item, UI and sales record aligned |
| Package dimensions and weight | Establishes the initial channel range |
| Package material and stiffness | Helps predict friction, bending and retention behavior |
| Channel assignment | Prevents refill and selection ambiguity |
| Units per channel | Supports realistic capacity and refill planning |
| Price and currency | Supports UI and payment configuration |
| Barcode or internal ID | Supports receiving and inventory mapping where used |
| Sample revision | Links compatibility evidence to the approved package |

Do not compress the layout simply to maximize a nominal selection count. Extra clearance or a different channel pitch may reduce headline capacity while improving package protection and delivery consistency. Capacity should be reported for the approved assortment, not as one universal number.

## Test delivery and protect the retail package

Agree what counts as a successful vend before production. The test should cover loading, retention, single-item separation, travel through the delivery path, detection where applicable and customer removal from the pickup area. After each vend, inspect the package for crushed corners, bent backing, torn film, opened seals, scuffing or product movement.

Use a repeatable test plan that includes:

1. each planned package family;
2. the dimensional and weight extremes;
3. representative upper, middle and lower channel positions;
4. repeated vends after normal refill loading;
5. empty-channel and sold-out behavior;
6. interruption and recovery behavior where power or network status affects the sale; and
7. the evidence and response required for a reported failed delivery.

A new package introduced later should be treated as a compatibility change. Similar front artwork does not prove that its thickness, stiffness or surface will behave the same. Use the general [capacity planning guide](/insights/vending-machine-capacity-planning-guide) to connect channel choices with refill frequency and product range.

## Design the merchandising and selection experience

The physical display, touchscreen listing, selection identifier and price must describe the same SKU. This is especially important when lash styles have similar packaging or differ only by curl, length, density, color or language. Define who supplies product images and descriptions, who approves price changes and how discontinued items are removed from the interface.

Confirm the required language, product hierarchy, image crop and sold-out message. If the platform allows remote catalog changes, use named roles and an approval process rather than one unrestricted account. A refill operator should be able to match the channel label to the software SKU without interpreting beauty marketing names from memory.

## Keep product compliance separate from machine configuration

The machine supplier does not replace the product brand's or seller's regulatory responsibilities. The product owner must determine classification, safety, labeling, warnings, language, traceability, import and retailer duties in every destination market.

For the United States, the FDA's [Eye Cosmetic Safety guidance](https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetic-products/eye-cosmetic-safety) states that false eyelashes, eyelash extensions and their adhesives are treated as cosmetic products and must meet applicable safety and labeling requirements. The FDA's [cosmetics labeling summary](https://www.fda.gov/cosmetics/cosmetics-labeling-regulations/summary-cosmetics-labeling-requirements) explains broader U.S. labeling responsibilities. These sources are U.S.-specific and do not prove compliance elsewhere. The buyer should obtain market-specific advice and provide BOHOL only the retail-ready products and display information approved for that market.

Avoid shared product testers inside an unattended machine unless the responsible product company and venue have a documented hygiene and legal basis. The vending specification should normally assume sealed retail units rather than opened samples.

## Map stock, refill and product changes

Define which events affect inventory: loading, successful vend, failed delivery, cancelled sale, refund, test vend, manual removal and stock correction. Decide whether inventory is managed by the machine platform, a separate operator system or a manual process, and identify the system of record.

The refill procedure should include channel identification, quantity loaded, package revision, operator identity and exception recording. For fast-changing assortments, define how the team approves a new product image, price, channel assignment and compatibility test before it becomes available for purchase. BOHOL's [inventory management guide](/insights/vending-machine-inventory-management-requirements) provides a broader event and alert framework.

## Confirm payment, refunds and connectivity by market

A reader or touchscreen shown in a machine photograph does not prove support for a particular bank, wallet, QR scheme or processor. Before the interface is frozen, document:

- display and transaction currency;
- required card, mobile-wallet, QR or NFC methods;
- terminal, gateway and acquiring provider;
- settlement currency, fees and exchange-rate ownership;
- local tax, receipt and fiscal requirements;
- SIM, Ethernet or Wi-Fi responsibility;
- offline, timeout and recovery behavior;
- authorization, cancellation, refund and failed-delivery workflow; and
- PCI DSS scope for the merchant, provider, integrator and connected systems.

The PCI Security Standards Council's [payment-terminal guidance](https://www.pcisecuritystandards.org/faqs/1300/) explains that terminals handling account data fall within the relevant cardholder-data environment and that the applicable controls depend on the implementation. The buyer must confirm provider approval and compliance scope with the acquirer or responsible payment organization. BOHOL needs the approved hardware and interface requirements but does not approve the merchant account or settlement arrangement. Use the [payment integration guide](/insights/vending-machine-payment-system-integration-guide) to assign these decisions.

## Check the venue and customer journey

Record the intended indoor or protected location, available floor area, delivery route, power, connectivity, ambient conditions, customer circulation, refill access and service clearance. Confirm that the open service door and refill activity will not block a required route. The venue operator should assess cabinet security, anchoring or anti-tip needs, keys, camera policy and incident response for the actual site.

Accessibility requirements depend on the destination and facility. Review the approach area, control reach, visual and language presentation, payment interaction and pickup access with the responsible local party. BOHOL's [accessibility procurement checklist](/insights/vending-machine-accessibility-procurement-checklist) helps capture these questions but is not a certification.

## Use a written OEM acceptance package

Before releasing production, attach these items to the approved order:

- controlled product schedule and representative samples;
- channel map and assortment-specific capacity;
- package-protection and repeated-vend acceptance method;
- screen, language, image, price and branding requirements;
- payment and connectivity responsibility matrix;
- inventory events, reports and user permissions;
- cabinet, power and site-interface drawing;
- delivery, installation, refill and service scope; and
- sample approval, change control and final acceptance records.

The [OEM/ODM process](/oem-odm) should start with these inputs rather than a request for a universal price or capacity. Final drawings, approved samples and provider confirmations control the project.

## Request an eyelash vending machine quotation

Send BOHOL the planned eyelash and beauty products, sealed-package dimensions and samples, target capacity, destination market, venue, currency, payment provider, connectivity, inventory workflow, branding files, estimated quantity and delivery requirement through the [contact page](/contact). BOHOL can review the product-to-channel schedule and identify the configuration, interfaces and tests needed for a quotation. Product compliance, payment acceptance and local installation approval remain the responsibility of the relevant market parties.
